    </ul>

    <h2>Royal Blizzard® Treats — Premium Tier</h2>
    <p>Royal Blizzards feature a warm filled center — fudge, caramel, peanut butter, or strawberry — that creates a burst of concentrated flavor when you hit the middle of the cup. They cost approximately $1 more than their standard counterparts per size. The lineup includes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Royal Oreo® Blizzard</strong> — Oreo pieces in soft serve with a hot fudge-filled center. The #1 Royal Blizzard. From $3.99.</li>
      <li><strong>Royal Reese's® Blizzard</strong> — Reese's PB cup pieces with a peanut butter-filled center. From $3.99.</li>
      <li><strong>Royal New York Cheesecake Blizzard</strong> — Graham cracker pieces and cheesecake with a strawberry-filled center. From $3.99.</li>
      <li><strong>Royal Rocky Road Trip Blizzard</strong> — Brownie pieces, almonds, marshmallow with a fudge-filled center. From $3.99.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Order the Best Blizzard</h2>
    <p>A few ordering tips from regulars that make a real difference:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Try the Mini before committing</strong> — If you've never had a flavor before, the Mini ($3.49–$3.99) is a low-risk way to test it. If you love it, you'll know to order a Medium next time.</li>
      <li><strong>Request extra mix-ins</strong> — Most locations charge about $0.50 for additional mix-ins. If you want more Oreo crunch or more cookie dough chunks per spoonful, ask.</li>
      <li><strong>Combine two flavors</strong> — DQ will blend two flavors together. The most popular combo is Oreo + Cookie Dough. Reese's + Brownie Batter is another fan favorite.</li>
      <li><strong>Upgrade to Royal</strong> — The Royal upgrade adds a warm filled center for about $1 more. The Royal Oreo with hot fudge center is the most-ordered upgrade on the entire Blizzard menu.</li>
      <li><strong>The upside-down flip</strong> — If the crew doesn't flip your Blizzard upside down before handing it over, it's free. The flip is also a quality check — a Blizzard thick enough to hold inverted is blended correctly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Blizzard vs. McFlurry vs. Frosty — What's the Difference?</h2>
    <p>The DQ Blizzard is frequently compared to McDonald's McFlurry and Wendy's Frosty. Here's the key distinction: the Blizzard is hand-blended to order, with mix-ins distributed throughout the entire cup. The McFlurry uses a machine that stirs mix-ins only at the surface. The Frosty is a standalone frozen beverage with no mix-ins. The Blizzard wins on texture depth and mix-in distribution.</p>

